## P18-V01 continuation

- Audited Java `MQClientInstance.consumeMessageDirectly` and both direct consume
  service implementations. Java accepts a `MessageClientExt` through its
  `MessageExt` parameter and preserves its runtime subclass for the listener.
  Rust had copied the effective and physical IDs but discarded that subtype.
- Commit `40a02244` retains the subtype marker across the base input and client
  decode, pull-result and admin-decode paths. Rust exposes the runtime-type ID
  lookup and honors the `MessageClientExt.setMsgId` no-op. The invoked Java
  fixture records callback class; the 26-case Rust direct-consume comparison
  checks class, effective ID, offset ID and offset isolation. Fixture SHA-256:
  `d0b4e39ba340e509d1ddc2665da122a0cf161ed757cc1d9ac2cbd022edb7d83f`.
- Strict Clippy initially rejected a `Default` field reassignment. The
  constructor was changed to a struct update initializer and committed as
  `6e2e3d09`; strict workspace Clippy then passed.
- Other public `MessageExt` subclasses, the full inherited/public/DTO/config and
  reverse-export audit, and remaining assignment/pull-expiry branches remain
  open P18-V01 work.

